      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Pointwise vs uniform continuity\n\n$f(x) = 1/x$ is continuous at every $x \\in (0, 1)$ — but **not uniformly** continuous. As $x_0 \\to 0^+$, the slope blows up; the same $\\varepsilon$ requires smaller and smaller $\\delta$. There's no single $\\delta$ that works for every $x_0$.\n\nQuantitative test: at $x_0 = 0.01$, the function value is $100$. To stay within $\\varepsilon = 1$, we need $|x - 0.01|$ tiny enough that $1/x$ doesn't change by more than 1. That's $\\delta \\approx \\varepsilon \\cdot x_0^2 = 10^{-4}$. As $x_0 \\to 0$, the required $\\delta$ shrinks to zero.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "x0_vals : [0.5, 0.1, 0.05, 0.01, 0.005]$\ndelta_required : map(lambda([x], 1.0 * x*x), x0_vals)$  /* δ ≈ ε·x₀² for ε=1 */\nprint(\"x₀:           \", x0_vals)$\nprint(\"δ required (ε=1):\", delta_required)$\nprint(\"→ δ shrinks to 0 with x₀, so no uniform δ works on (0, 1)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 26,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "x₀:            [0.5,0.1,0.05,0.01,0.005]\nδ required (ε=1):\n                 [0.25,0.010000000000000002,0.0025000000000000005,1.0e-4,\n                  2.5e-5]\n→ δ shrinks to 0 with x₀, so no uniform δ works on (0, 1)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "On a closed bounded interval $[a, b]$ with $a > 0$, however, $1/x$ **is** uniformly continuous — by the Heine-Cantor theorem, every continuous function on a compact set is uniformly continuous. The pathology only appears when the interval reaches the singularity.\n\n## Lipschitz and Hölder continuity\n\nStronger conditions. $f$ is **Lipschitz** with constant $L$ if $|f(x) - f(y)| \\le L\\,|x - y|$ for all $x, y$. **Hölder of order $\\alpha \\in (0, 1]$** is $|f(x) - f(y)| \\le C\\,|x - y|^\\alpha$.\n\nLipschitz ⇒ uniformly continuous; the converse fails (e.g. $\\sqrt{x}$ on $[0, 1]$ is Hölder of order $1/2$ but not Lipschitz, because the derivative blows up at 0).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "x_sqrt : np_to_list(np_linspace(0, 1, 200))$\ny_sqrt : map(lambda([x], float(sqrt(x))), x_sqrt)$\ny_lin  : map(lambda([x], float(x)), x_sqrt)$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"blue\", line_width=2, name=\"√x (Hölder ½, not Lipschitz)\",\n  lines(x_sqrt, y_sqrt),\n  color=\"green\", line_width=2, name=\"x (Lipschitz with L=1)\",\n  lines(x_sqrt, y_lin),\n  title=\"Vertical tangent at 0 → not Lipschitz, but still uniformly continuous\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"f(x)\",\n  grid=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 27,
      "outputs": [
        {
