    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Why uniform continuity matters\n\nSeveral big theorems require it:\n\n- **Riemann integrability**: every continuous function on $[a, b]$ is integrable, because compact ⇒ uniformly continuous (Heine-Cantor).\n- **Exchange of limits**: $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\lim_{x\\to x_0} f_n(x) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0} \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} f_n(x)$ requires uniform convergence (next notebook).\n- **Numerical stability**: uniformly continuous functions tolerate small input perturbations with bounded error.\n\nPointwise continuity is enough for *evaluating* a function, but uniform continuity is what makes analysis hang together.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}